WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.569
Hello!

2
00:00:00.569 --> 00:00:04.176
We now move to higher-order equations.

3
00:00:04.176 --> 00:00:12.433
When a phone falls, its height satisfies the equation second derivative equals minus g.

4
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:19.646
We will see how to solve such equations and when their order can be reduced.

5
00:00:19.896 --> 00:00:25.694
The general solution of an n-th order equation has n arbitrary constants.

6
00:00:25.694 --> 00:00:34.906
In the Cauchy problem n conditions are given at one point: the function and its derivatives up to order n minus one.

7
00:00:34.906 --> 00:00:41.021
If the right side and its partial derivatives in the unknowns are continuous,

8
00:00:41.021 --> 00:00:44.198
a solution exists locally and is unique.

9
00:00:44.448 --> 00:00:48.694
Type one: the n-th derivative depends only on x.

10
00:00:48.694 --> 00:00:56.479
For example, y triple prime equals twenty-four x, with initial values one, zero and two.

11
00:00:56.479 --> 00:01:04.795
Integrate and find each constant immediately: y double prime equals twelve x squared plus two,

12
00:01:04.795 --> 00:01:13.022
y prime equals four x cubed plus two x, and y equals x to the fourth plus x squared plus one.

13
00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:16.603
Type two: an equation without y.

14
00:01:16.603 --> 00:01:23.577
For example, x y double prime minus y prime equals three x squared.

15
00:01:23.577 --> 00:01:30.759
Putting z equal to y prime gives a first-order linear equation for z.

16
00:01:30.759 --> 00:01:37.317
Dividing by x squared, the derivative of z over x equals three.

17
00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:47.726
So z equals three x squared plus C one x, and y equals x cubed plus half C one x squared plus C two.

18
00:01:47.976 --> 00:01:55.970
Type three: an equation without x, such as y double prime equals two y cubed.

19
00:01:55.970 --> 00:02:00.849
Put y prime equal to p, with p a function of y.

20
00:02:00.849 --> 00:02:06.767
By the chain rule y double prime equals p times dp by dy.

21
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:09.777
Important: not just dp by dy!

22
00:02:09.777 --> 00:02:16.214
Integrating gives p squared equals y to the fourth plus C one.

23
00:02:16.464 --> 00:02:25.030
Take the initial values y of zero equals one and y prime of zero equals one.

24
00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.764
Then C one is zero and p equals y squared.

25
00:02:29.764 --> 00:02:39.006
Separating, minus one over y equals x minus one, so y equals one over one minus x.

26
00:02:39.006 --> 00:02:47.798
The solution exists only for x less than one; as x approaches one it blows up.

27
00:02:48.048 --> 00:02:53.582
The graph shows y equals one over one minus x.

28
00:02:53.582 --> 00:02:58.034
At zero it equals one with slope one.

29
00:02:58.034 --> 00:03:06.455
As x approaches one, the curve shoots up towards a vertical asymptote.

30
00:03:06.455 --> 00:03:12.110
Such blow-up is common for nonlinear equations.

31
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:18.784
Check yourself: how is y double prime expressed for an equation without x?

32
00:03:18.784 --> 00:03:22.430
Choose an answer and read the explanation.
